cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "titer: measuring people-search claims against attested records"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Theodoro
    given-names: Caio
repository-code: "https://github.com/caiotheodoro/titer"
url: "https://huggingface.co/spaces/caiotheodoro/titer"
abstract: >-
  An instrument for measuring whether a people-search provider's claim about a
  person is attested by a public record rather than judged by a model. Ground
  truth comes from SEC Section 16 filings and from OpenAlex authorship backed by
  Crossref DOIs; no model assigns any label. Six headline measurements were
  produced; five turned out to be artefacts of the instrument rather than
  findings about any vendor, each caught by reading returned rows rather than a
  summary statistic. The surviving result, confirmed three times on negatives
  verified exhaustively before the provider was asked, is that one provider
  confirms attested expertise 96% of the time while affirming claims about
  topics a person has never published in one time in six for an adjacent
  subfield and one time in twelve for a wholly different domain, at 0.987
  stated confidence against 0.889 accuracy. The control rate does not reach
  zero, which bounds the prompt-interpretation objection rather than leaving it
  open. Two formal retractions are published alongside.
keywords:
  - entity resolution
  - expertise verification
  - benchmark auditing
  - measurement error
  - people search
license: MIT
date-released: "2026-09-04"
references:
